Functional Abdominal Bloating
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Sensation of abdominal pressure, Visible daily increasing girth, Absence of pain/altered bowel habits

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-021
Reason for Visit: Acute distress and severe anxiety due to massive, progressive abdominal swelling over the past 12 hours leading to difficulty taking deep breaths.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient only.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated severe, progressive abdominal distension causing significant mechanical respiratory discomfort and extreme patient distress. Urgent face-to-face assessment was required to definitively rule out acute small bowel obstruction or rapidly accumulating ascites, preventing potential respiratory compromise or ischemic bowel events prior to the next available in-hours consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach has swelled up like a balloon since lunchtime, the pressure is immense, and I feel like I cannot draw a full breath."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a recurrent subjective sensation of abdominal fullness, pressure, and trapped gas that began insidiously 3 months ago. Today, there has been a dramatic, visible objective increase in abdominal girth progressing rapidly throughout the day following a high-carbohydrate lunch. Patient notes worsening of symptoms strictly after meals. Historically reports relief of discomfort overnight or upon passing flatus. There is an absence of significant crampy abdominal pain dominating the clinical picture, and normal bowel habits are maintained without chronic diarrhoea or severe constipation. Denies unintentional weight loss, rectal bleeding, or nocturnal awakenings. Exacerbation is clearly linked to recent severe occupational stress and consumption of highly fermentable carbohydrates.
Functional Status: Ambulating independently but unable to wear regular clothing due to acute distension; adopting a semi-recumbent position to breathe comfortably.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute anxiety regarding the possibility of an intra-abdominal tumor or surgical emergency.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient appears highly anxious, frequently rubbing the abdomen, and wearing unbuttoned trousers. No overt respiratory distress or cyanosis.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is visibly distended and globally tympanic to percussion. No palpable organomegaly or discrete masses. Active, normal-pitched bowel sounds are present throughout all four quadrants. Abdomen is soft and non-tender without guarding or rebound peritonism. The clinical presentation explicitly aligns with the sensation of abdominal pressure, visible daily increasing girth, and absence of pain/altered bowel habits.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty wrappers for artificial-sweetener-based dietetic snacks on the desk.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular prescription medications. Has taken multiple over-the-counter simethicone capsules today without relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Functional Abdominal Bloating - Status: Exacerbated (Acute severe distension episode)
Differentials:

Small Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of normal bowel movements earlier today, active normal-pitched bowel sounds, and the total absence of severe, colicky abdominal pain or bilious vomiting.

Ovarian Malignancy (Ascites) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The tympanic (gas-filled) rather than dull percussion note, absence of a palpable pelvic mass, and the diurnal pattern of symptoms resolving entirely overnight.

Coeliac Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The explicit absence of chronic diarrhoea, steatorrhoea, unintentional weight loss, or refractory iron deficiency anaemia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe abdominal distension causing mechanical discomfort and high clinical anxiety. The speed of the visible swelling mandated urgent out-of-hours evaluation to definitively exclude a surgical crisis such as a mechanical bowel obstruction or rapidly expanding intra-abdominal pathology. Clinical examination firmly established severe functional gaseous distension (tympanitic, soft abdomen with normal bowel sounds) and excluded peritonism or fluid accumulation. The exclusion of an acute surgical abdomen immediately de-escalated the emergency, preventing an unnecessary and highly costly presentation to the Emergency Department overnight. Reassurance and antispasmodic therapy were safely initiated.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Mebeverine 135mg to be taken 20 minutes before meals. Advised cessation of artificial sweeteners.
Positive Behavioral Change: When eating meals, deliberately put your fork down between every single bite and chew the food until it is entirely liquid. This physical action prevents you from unconsciously swallowing large volumes of air and thoroughly mixes saliva into the food, directly reducing the fermentation and gas production that causes your severe abdominal swelling.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to discuss a low-FODMAP dietary trial and routine screening bloods.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the abdomen becomes rigid or board-like, if severe, sharp, unrelenting pain develops, or if uncontrollable vomiting begins, present immediately to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis assumes no structural abnormalities; routine blood tests and potentially an ultrasound may be required by your regular doctor to be absolutely certain.
Clinical Course: The extreme bloating will subside completely by morning. Long-term management requires strict dietary modification and stress reduction.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The physical examination today showed no signs of a burst bowel or blockage. However, if you develop a high fever, start vomiting persistently, or if the pain suddenly becomes agonizing, you must seek emergency medical care.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your bowel is structurally normal, but it is highly sensitive to the gas produced when normal bacteria digest certain carbohydrates and artificial sweeteners. The extreme distension is a severe muscle reflex trapping this gas. The prescribed medication will relax the smooth muscle of the bowel, allowing the trapped gas to dissipate naturally.
Actionable Steps: Eliminate all sugar-free gums, diet sodas, and artificial sweeteners from your diet immediately. These compounds are indigestible by your body but rapidly fermented by gut bacteria, creating the exact high-volume gas causing your current distress.

